WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.539
Reliability compounds: the arithmetic.

2
00:00:02.819 --> 00:00:10.328
Suppose an agent completes a task in n steps, each step succeeds with probability p, and steps fail independently.

3
00:00:10.608 --> 00:00:13.701
Then the whole task succeeds with probability p to the n.

4
00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:18.291
That one formula explains most of the gap between demos and production.

5
00:00:18.571 --> 00:00:19.659
Plug in numbers.

6
00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:21.325
At p = 0.

7
00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:28.069
95, ten steps succeed about 60% of the time and twenty steps about 36%.

8
00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:29.736
At p = 0.

9
00:00:30.016 --> 00:00:33.408
99, twenty steps succeed about 82%.

10
00:00:33.688 --> 00:00:41.517
Inverting the formula, a target success rate T over n steps requires p = T to the power 1/n per step.

11
00:00:41.797 --> 00:00:47.301
To finish a 20-step task 90% of the time, each step must succeed about 99.

12
00:00:47.581 --> 00:00:49.395
47% of the time.

13
00:00:49.675 --> 00:00:53.557
For 99% end to end, each step needs about 99.

14
00:00:53.837 --> 00:00:55.181
95%.

15
00:00:55.461 --> 00:01:04.635
Taking logs makes the scaling clear: the log of success is n times the log of p, so the failure budget per step shrinks roughly as one over n.

16
00:01:04.915 --> 00:01:09.693
Doubling the length of a task roughly halves the per-step error rate you can tolerate.

17
00:01:09.973 --> 00:01:15.477
A workflow is subject to the same arithmetic, which is why lesson 2 put gates between steps.

18
00:01:15.757 --> 00:01:17.891
The difference is what you can do about it.

19
00:01:18.171 --> 00:01:23.632
In a workflow you know the steps, so you can measure each one, test it, and add a check where it is weak.

20
00:01:23.912 --> 00:01:31.400
In an agent the number of steps varies by run, and the model chooses them, so neither n nor the per-step p is under your control.

21
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.203
Every additional step the model decides to take is another factor less than one.
